I like to call this bare minimum checklist that can keep your server safe and help you carry out proper system Audit.

[ ] 1. Identify OS, version, kernel and architecture
[ ] 2. Identify server role and required services
[ ] 3. Establish current security baseline
[ ] 4. Run vulnerability/security assessment
[ ] 5. Review installed packages
[ ] 6. Review running services
[ ] 7. Review listening ports
[ ] 8. Review network interfaces and routes
[ ] 9. Configure host firewall
[ ] 10. Establish deny-by-default inbound policy
[ ] 11. Permit only required services/ports
[ ] 12. Harden SSH
[ ] 13. Disable unnecessary SSH authentication methods
[ ] 14. Restrict root remote access
[ ] 15. Enforce SSH key authentication where appropriate
[ ] 16. Configure authentication/PAM controls
[ ] 17. Configure brute-force/account lockout protection
[ ] 18. Review users and groups
[ ] 19. Review sudo privileges
[ ] 20. Review password policy
[ ] 21. Enable/verify AppArmor or another MAC framework
[ ] 22. Configure auditd
[ ] 23. Verify audit events are actually generated
[ ] 24. Deploy file-integrity monitoring
[ ] 25. Define AIDE monitoring scope
[ ] 26. Create trusted AIDE baseline
[ ] 27. Test AIDE change detection
[ ] 28. Review SUID/SGID executables
[ ] 29. Review world-writable files/directories
[ ] 30. Harden kernel parameters
[ ] 31. Review kernel lockdown requirements
[ ] 32. Review systemd service security
[ ] 33. Disable unnecessary services
[ ] 34. Review scheduled tasks/cron
[ ] 35. Review boot configuration
[ ] 36. Review file ownership and permissions
[ ] 37. Review logging configuration
[ ] 38. Review log retention
[ ] 39. Review security-update strategy
[ ] 40. Perform vulnerability scanning
[ ] 41. Perform network/service validation
[ ] 42. Re-run security assessment
[ ] 43. Compare before/after results
[ ] 44. Validate controls behaviorally
[ ] 45. Record remaining risks and exceptions
